      "documentConcept": "PD subtypes and precision medicine",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:f27ce994e068643549fcbf78b81056f3e4d59b0d98dbedb6aba7ed7b78ad15f9",
      "text": "Studies in different cohorts report that Parkinson's disease subtypes differ in progression, and authors link the failure of disease-modifying trials in part to heterogeneity. Published schemes include data-driven clusters and the SynNeurGe research classification, which combines pathological alpha-synuclein, neurodegeneration defined by neuroimaging and pathogenic gene variants. Authors report that replication of published subtype classifications is limited. A genetic consortium preprint reports many potentially trial-eligible carriers of GBA1 and LRRK2 variants. Sponsors state that the LRRK2 kinase inhibitor BIIB122 did not meet its endpoints in an early-stage study that included people with or without a pathogenic LRRK2 variant and that they will discontinue development in idiopathic disease, while a study in carriers continues. Reports differ on motor progression in seed amplification assay negative disease.",
      "sources": [
